 Ansible Variables: group_vars, host_vars, extra vars & precedence

As your Ansible usage grows, you need better ways to organize variables.
That’s where group_vars, host_vars, and extra vars come in.

🔹 group_vars

Variables defined for a group of servers.

Use case:
 When multiple servers share the same configuration.

Example idea:
All web servers use the same port or package name.
Defined once, applied to all hosts in that group.

🔹 host_vars

Variables defined for a single specific server.

Use case:
When one host needs a different value than the rest of the group.
These variables override group-level values for that host.

🔹 extra vars

Variables passed at runtime using the command line.

Use case:
Temporary overrides or environment-specific values.
They are not stored in files and exist only for that execution.

🔹 Variable precedence (most important concept)

When the same variable is defined in multiple places, Ansible follows a priority order.

Simplified rule to remember:

More specific wins over less specific.

General idea of precedence:
group_vars → host_vars → extra vars
Extra vars always have the highest priority.

🔹 Simple mental model

 group_vars apply broadly.
 host_vars customize a single server.
 extra vars override everything temporarily.
